  DOM-and-error-display boilerplate is the part that will actually hurt across five forms, and layering it keeps the
  validation logic itself dependency-free and unit-testable. But it is the most code, so I want your call.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should the reusable piece cover? → Core + thin binder (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: how a form declares what it needs. This one is the public interface — it's what every future form writes, so
  changing it later means rewriting all of them.

  - [1mDeclarative schema object[0m — [38;5;153m{ username: { required: true, minLength: 3 }, email: { required: true, email: true } }[39m.
    Buys the most readable form definitions and makes rules data (serializable, inspectable). Costs: anything unusual
    needs a new built-in rule name added to the core, or an escape hatch.
  - [1mComposable validator functions[0m — [38;5;153m{ username: [required(), minLength(3)] }[39m, where each validator is a small function
    returning an error or null. Buys unlimited extensibility with zero core changes — a custom rule is just a function
    you write locally. Costs: slightly noisier to read, and rules aren't plain data.
  - [1mHybrid[0m — built-in rules ship as factory functions, so the common case reads like the schema form but a bare function
    is always accepted in the same array.

  [1mMy recommendation: composable validator functions (the hybrid shape).[0m With unknown future forms, the rule you'll need
  next is the one I didn't anticipate, and the function form means that costs you a local three-line function instead of
  an edit to the shared core. The built-in factories keep the common case terse.
